==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|afflicten}} (attested in past participle {{m|enm|afflicte}}), from {{der|en|la|afflictāre|t=to damage, harass, torment}}, frequentative of {{m|la|affligere||to dash down, overthrow}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/əˈflɪkt/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-afflict.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪkt|s=2}}
* {{hyph|en|af|flict}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To cause (someone) [[pain]], [[suffering]] or [[distress]].
#* {{RQ:KJV|Exodus|1|11–12|text=Therefore they did set over them taskmasters to '''afflict''' them with their burdens. And they built for Pharaoh treasure cities, Pithom and Raamses. But the more they '''afflicted''' them, the more they multiplied and grew. And they were grieved because of the children of Israel.}}
#* {{RQ:KJV|Leviticus|23|27|text=Also on the tenth day of this seventh month there shall be a day of atonement: it shall be an holy convocation unto you; and ye shall '''afflict''' your souls, and offer an offering made by fire unto the {{smallcaps|Lord}}.}}
#* {{RQ:Lindsay Redheap|page=41|passage=[T]he wench was '''afflicted''' with religion and unapproachably austere.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To strike or cast down; to overthrow.
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|book=1|passage=reassembling our '''afflicted''' powers|year=1873}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To make low or humble.
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|1|passage=The Argument of mine '''afflicted''' stile}}
#* {{RQ:Taylor Holy Living
|passage=Men are apt to prefer a prosperous error before an '''afflicted''' truth.}}

